const lastReviewed = 'July 2026';

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'When does Medicare Part B coverage start if I enroll before turning 65?',
    answer: 'According to the Social Security Administration, if you enroll in Medicare Part B during the three months before your 65th birth month, or during your actual birth month, your coverage begins on the first day of the month you turn 65. If your birthday is on the first of the month, coverage starts one month earlier — on the first of the preceding month.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is the "born on the first" Medicare rule?',
    answer: 'Social Security rules state that if your birthday falls on the first day of any month — for example, October 1 — your Medicare Initial Enrollment Period (IEP) and coverage effective dates are calculated as if you turned 65 in the preceding month (September). This means your IEP starts three months earlier than it would for someone born on October 2.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What happens if I enroll in Medicare during the three months after my birth month?',
    answer: 'Under rules updated by the Consolidated Appropriations Act of 2021, if you enroll during the three months after your 65th birth month (months 5, 6, or 7 of your Initial Enrollment Period), your Medicare coverage begins on the first day of the month following the month you enrolled. Previously there was a longer delay — this rule was fixed to reduce coverage gaps.',
  },
  {
    question: 'When does General Enrollment Period (GEP) coverage start?',
    answer: 'The General Enrollment Period (GEP) runs from January 1 through March 31 each year and is for people who missed their Initial Enrollment Period and do not qualify for a Special Enrollment Period. Coverage now begins on the first day of the month following your enrollment month. The old rule forcing a wait until July 1 was eliminated by the Consolidated Appropriations Act of 2021.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is Medicare Part A retroactive when I apply after age 65?',
    answer: 'Yes. When you apply for Medicare Part A (hospital insurance) after turning 65, your Part A coverage can be backdated — also called retroactive coverage — by up to six months from your application date, but no earlier than the month you first became eligible (the month you turned 65). This retroactivity does not apply to Part B.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is the Special Enrollment Period (SEP) for Medicare?',
    answer: 'A Special Enrollment Period (SEP) is an 8-month window to enroll in Medicare Part B without penalty after your active employment ends or your group health coverage through a current employer ends — whichever comes first. During this 8-month window your coverage starts on the first of the month following the month you enroll. If you miss the SEP, your coverage does not start until the next GEP period.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Why does the specific month I enroll in Medicare matter so much?',
    answer: 'The month you enroll in Medicare determines the exact date your doctor and hospital coverage begins. Enrolling one month too late during the Initial Enrollment Period shifts your start date by an entire month and could leave you with a dangerous gap in coverage — especially if you are transitioning off employer health insurance. Use this calculator to find your exact start date before leaving employer coverage.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I delay Medicare Part B past 65 without a penalty if I am still working?',
    answer: 'Yes, but only if you have active group health coverage through your own current employer or a working spouse\'s current employer with 20 or more employees. If your employer has fewer than 20 employees, Medicare is your primary payer at 65 and you must enroll to avoid the permanent late enrollment penalty. Always confirm your employer\'s size with HR before delaying Part B.',
